  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated thunderstorms possible late this afternoon. \nScattered showers regionwide tonight, with isolated\nthunderstorms possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA small, weak jet streak will develop aloft over the Gulf of\nMaine, with our forecast area sitting in the left exit region.\nThese dynamics will support the development of some brief vort\nmax this afternoon which, in addition to peak diurnal heating,\ncould provide enough lift and instability to support isolated to\nscattered showers and storms.\n\nMost CAMs guidance and subsequently the NBM suggest a start time\nfor precip in the CWA to begin around 00z tonight within the\nCentral Highlands. However, the NAMNEST is an outlier, bringing\nshower development as early as around 16z. With this forecast,\nthe start time to PoPs was sped up by a few hours to account for\nthis uncertainty, though it is possible if the NAMNEST solution\nis correct that precip could start even earlier.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms on Friday, some of \nwhich may be strong with gusty winds and small hail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA stronger, more robust jet streak will develop aloft over the\nGulf of Maine on Friday, with our forecast area once again\nsitting in the left exit region. A cold front will push through\nthe forecast area on Friday, bringing synoptic lift strong\nenough for scattered to numerous showers and the development of\nthunderstorms by the afternoon.\n\nRecent runs of the HREF and REFS indicate moderate shear of 25\nto 30 kts, along with generous PWATs of 1.0 to 1.5 inches, with\nhigher moisture content being within the marine layer near the\ncoast. What seems more up in the air is the amount of CAPE, with\nthe HREF mean indicating 1000 to 1500 J/kg of SBCAPE, while the\nREFS has less with around 500 to 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE. The amount\nof CAPE may come down to how much high cloud cover is in the\narea through the day on Friday, with more cloud cover\npotentially limiting how much the atmosphere is able to charge\nbefore storms fire. With higher CAPE values, in combination with\nthe shear available, there could be the threat for some stronger\nthunderstorms that may produce gusty winds or even some small\nhail, though freezing levels may lift to 10 to 11 kft, allowing\nfor ample melting of any hail to push threats more in the\ndirection of gusty winds. Additionally, forecast profiles\nindicate most of the CAPE profile could be above the saturated\nlayer, which is another indicator that any strong to severe\nthreat will be in the form of wind.\n\nKey Message 3...System bringing rain showers and chance for\nthunder moves in Sunday afternoon. \n\nLow pressure system in Canada moves eastward this weekend, with\ntrough axis moving through Sunday afternoon. Associated cold \nfront moves through the state Sunday, funneling in some cooler \nair behind it. Rain moves into the north, generally expanding \nsoutheastward during the day Sunday. PWATs generally around \n1-1.50 inches based on global model outputs, so currently not \nanticipating any rain totals over one quarter inch. There will \nbe a few hundred joules of CAPE, 40-50 kts of effective shear, \nand some decent low level lapse rates. Combined, there is enough\ninstability that a thunderstorm is not out of the question. \nHeating ahead of the cold front in eastern Maine could aid in \ninitiating some convection along the boundary, but not \nanticipating severe storms at this time.",
